For all other edges, the initial flow values are simply the half of the edge capacities, so we do not need to store them, as we read in the edge capacity every time we see an edge.

Now, it is clear that the construction itself is feasible, so we need to show that it is centered. \[BLL<sup>+</sup>21\] also proves centrality.

**Lemma 6.2** (Initial point for the modified min cost flow, Lemma 7.5 of \[BLL<sup>+</sup>21\])**.** _Let_ \(\left[\begin{array}{ c }
s^{(\mathrm{init})} \\
\widetilde{s}^{(\mathrm{init})}
\end{array}\right]=\left[\begin{array}{ c }
c \\
\widetilde{c}
\end{array}\right]\) _and_ \(\mu^{(\mathrm{init})}=100m^2W^3\epsilon^{-1}\)_. Then, the point_ \(\left(\left[\begin{array}{ c }
x^{(\mathrm{init})} \\
\widetilde{x}^{(\mathrm{init})}
\end{array}\right],\left[\begin{array}{ c }
s^{(\mathrm{init})} \\
\widetilde{s}^{(\mathrm{init})}
\end{array}\right],\mu^{(\mathrm{init})}\right)\) _is_ \(\epsilon\)_-centered w.r.t. the linear program in_ (15).

Thus this is a suitable initial point for our algorithm.

Finally, we need to show that this modified flow does indeed solve the original flow problem. Let \(\mathrm{OPT}(\widetilde{G})\) be the optimal value of the above LP. Since the star-edge have cost \(>m\|c\|_\infty\), any flow that goes along these edges, i.e., some (_v,z),(z,w_), would be cheaper if it was instead routed along any other _vw_-path using only original edges. So the optimal solution does not use these edges, and thus the optimal flow on the original graph and the new graph is identical. This assumes that it is possible to re-route the flow. If that is impossible, then that would be there is no path from _v_ to _w_ in the residual graph and thus the demand of the original problem cannot be satisfied.

**Fact 6.3.** \(\mathrm{OPT}(\widetilde{G})=\mathrm{OPT}(G)\).

## 6.2 Final Point & Existence of Nearby Exact Solution

We now need to show that after running our algorithm we have indeed found a near optimal solution.

We prove the following lemma.

**Lemma 6.4.** _Given an_ \(\epsilon\)_-centered point_ \(\left(\left[\begin{array}{ c }
x \\
\widetilde{x}
\end{array}\right],\left[\begin{array}{ c }
s \\
\widetilde{s}
\end{array}\right],\mu\right)\) _of_ (15) _where_ \(\mu<\epsilon/n^2\)_, there exists x_<sup>OPT</sup> _which is an optimal flow for G for which for_ \(\widetilde{x}^\mathrm{OPT}=0\) _we have_ \(\|\left[\begin{array}{ c }
x \\
\widetilde{x}
\end{array}\right]-\left[\begin{array}{ c }
x^\mathrm{OPT} \\
\widetilde{x}^\mathrm{OPT}
\end{array}\right]\|_\infty\lesssim\epsilon W\).

_Proof._ Note from Fact 6.3 we have _x_<sup>OPT</sup> being an optimal flow for _G_ is equivalent to \(\left[\begin{array}{ c }
x^\mathrm{OPT} \\
\widetilde{x}^\mathrm{OPT}
\end{array}\right]\) being an optimal flow for \(\widetilde{G}\). Then, this lemma follows directly from Lemmas 6.5 and 6.7 when taking \(\mu\le\frac{\epsilon}{n^2}\).

**Lemma 6.5** (Extension of Lemma 7.7 of \[BLL<sup>+</sup>21\])**.** _Given an_ \(\epsilon\)_-centered point_ \(\left(\left[\begin{array}{ c }
x \\
\widetilde{x}
\end{array}\right],\left[\begin{array}{ c }
s \\
\widetilde{s}
\end{array}\right],\mu\right)\) _of_ (15) _where_ \(\mu<1/n\)_, there exists_ \(\left[\begin{array}{ c }
x^{(\mathrm{final})} \\
\widetilde{x}^{(\mathrm{final})}
\end{array}\right]\) _which is a feasible flow for_ \(\widetilde{G}\) _where_ \(c^\top x^{(\mathrm{final})}+\widetilde{c}^\top\widetilde{x}^{(\mathrm{final})}\le\mathrm{OPT}(\widetilde{G})+\mu n\)_. Moreover,_ \(\|\left[\begin{array}{ c }
x \\
\widetilde{x}
\end{array}\right]-\left[\begin{array}{ c }
x^{(\mathrm{final})} \\
\widetilde{x}^{(\mathrm{final})}
\end{array}\right]\|_\infty\lesssim\epsilon W\).

_Proof._ The first part of the lemma is simply Lemma 7.7 of \[BLL<sup>+</sup>21\].<br>
To show the moreover part, we use the following claim:

**Claim 6.6** (Claim A.6 of \[BLL<sup>+</sup>21\])**.**<tbc>

\(\left\|\Phi''\left(\left[\begin{array}{ c }
x \\
\widetilde{x}
\end{array}\right]\right)^\frac{1}{2}\left(\left[\begin{array}{ c }
x^{(\mathrm{final})} \\
\widetilde{x}^{(\mathrm{final})}
\end{array}\right]-\left[\begin{array}{ c }
x \\
\widetilde{x}
\end{array}\right]\right)\right\|_\infty\lesssim\epsilon\)

<tbc>Then we have for any edge _e_ (including star edges) \(\phi''(x_e)=\left(\frac{1}{x_e^2}-\frac{1}{(u_e-x_e)^2}\right)\lesssim\epsilon\). Recall \(0\le x_e\le u_e\le W\). Then

\(|x_e^{(\mathrm{final})}-x_e|\lesssim\frac{\epsilon}{(1/x_e^2+1/(u_e-x_e)^2)^{1/2}}\le\frac{\epsilon}{(2/W^2)^{1/2}}\le\epsilon W.\)

where the second step follows from \(1/x_e^2+1/(u_e-x_e)^2\ge 2/W^2\) for all \(0\le x_e\le u_w\le W\) (see Fact B.1) and the last step follows from simple algebra.

Now we need to show that this final point is also close to the exact optimal point to conclude that the point found by the IPM is too. Note that it suffices to show that \(\left[\begin{array}{ c }
x^{(\mathrm{final})} \\
\widetilde{x}^{(\mathrm{final})}
\end{array}\right]\) is close to an optimal point of \(\widetilde{G}\) as any optimal flow for \(\widetilde{G}\) would not use any of the new star edges.

**Lemma 6.7.** _Let_ \(\left[\begin{array}{ c }
x^{(\mathrm{final})} \\
\widetilde{x}^{(\mathrm{final})}
\end{array}\right]\) _be a feasible flow for_ \(\widetilde{G}\) _where_ \(c^\top x^{(\mathrm{final})}+\widetilde{c}^\top\widetilde{x}^{(\mathrm{final})}\le\mathrm{OPT}(\widetilde{G})+\mu n\)_. Then there exists an optimal flow_ \(\left[\begin{array}{ c }
x^\mathrm{OPT} \\
\widetilde{x}^\mathrm{OPT}
\end{array}\right]\) _of_ \(\widetilde{G}\) _with_ \(\widetilde{x}^\mathrm{OPT}=0\) _such that_ \(\|\left[\begin{array}{ c }
x^{(\mathrm{final})} \\
\widetilde{x}^{(\mathrm{final})}
\end{array}\right]-\left[\begin{array}{ c }
x^\mathrm{OPT} \\
\widetilde{x}^\mathrm{OPT}
\end{array}\right]\|_\infty\le\mu n^2\).

_Proof._ \(\left[\begin{array}{ c }
x^{(\mathrm{final})} \\
\widetilde{x}^{(\mathrm{final})}
\end{array}\right]\) is feasible but not optimal. Recall that we can get an optimal flow by repeatedly improving the value of a feasible flow by finding a negative cost cycle in the residual graph induced by this flow, then augmenting the flow by this cycle. Let \(\left[\begin{array}{ c }
x^\mathrm{OPT} \\
\widetilde{x}^\mathrm{OPT}
\end{array}\right]\) be the optimal flow created by augmenting \(\left[\begin{array}{ c }
x^{(\mathrm{final})} \\
\widetilde{x}^{(\mathrm{final})}
\end{array}\right]\) in this way.<br>
Note that our edge costs are integral; thus, each cycle has cost at most -1. Since we have \(c^\top x^{(\mathrm{final})}+\widetilde{c}^\top\widetilde{x}^{(\mathrm{final})}\le\mathrm{OPT}(\widetilde{G})+\mu n\), and each cycle can only use _n_ edges, we have \(\|\left[\begin{array}{ c }
x^{(\mathrm{final})} \\
\widetilde{x}^{(\mathrm{final})}
\end{array}\right]-\left[\begin{array}{ c }
x^\mathrm{OPT} \\
\widetilde{x}^\mathrm{OPT}
\end{array}\right]\|_\infty\le\|\left[\begin{array}{ c }
x^{(\mathrm{final})} \\
\widetilde{x}^{(\mathrm{final})}
\end{array}\right]-\left[\begin{array}{ c }
x^\mathrm{OPT} \\
\widetilde{x}^\mathrm{OPT}
\end{array}\right]\|_1\le\mu n^2\).

## 6.3 Putting Everything Together

In this section, we prove the main result Theorems 1.1 and 1.2.

**Theorem 1.1** (Min-cost Flow, Streaming)**.** _There is a randomized multi-pass streaming algorithm that, given an input graph G_=(_V,E_) _with integer capacities_ \(u\in[1,W]^E\) _and costs_ \(c\in[1,W]^E\)_, vertex demand vector_ \(b\in\mathbb{R}^V\) _and accuracy parameter_ \(\epsilon\in[0,1]\)_, computes w.h.p. a min-cost flow in_ \(\widetilde{O}(n^{1.5}\mathrm{log}(W/\epsilon))\) _space and_ \(\widetilde{O}(\sqrt{n}\mathrm{log}(W/\epsilon))\) _passes over the input. The total time is_ \(\widetilde{O}(mn\mathrm{log}^2(W/\epsilon))\).

_The algorithm returns a randomized_ \(\widetilde{O}(n^{1.5}\mathrm{log}(W/\epsilon))\)_-space data structure that supports edge-queries: For any given edge e_=((_w,v),c<sub>e</sub>,u<sub>e</sub>), the query returns in_ \(\widetilde{O}(\sqrt{n}\mathrm{log}(W/\epsilon))\) _time an estimate <o>f</o><sub>e</sub> of the flow on that edge, with_ \(0\le\overline{f_e}\le u_e\) _and_ \(|\overline{f_e}-f_e|\le\epsilon\) _with high probability._

_Alternatively, we can also assume that during the last pass, the algorithm returns for each given edge e_=((_w,v),c<sub>e</sub>,u<sub>e</sub>_) _the flow <o>f</o><sub>e</sub> on that edge._

_Proof._ We use the interior point method Algorithm 1, specifically Theorem 4.1. This method requires an \(\epsilon\)-centered initial point \((x,s,\mu)\) with query time _T_<sub>0</sub>, and a target \(\mu^{(\mathrm{target})}\).

We use Lemma 6.1 to find an \(\epsilon/W\)-centered initial point, which also gives us a \(T_0=\widetilde{O}(1)\) initial query time. By Lemma 6.2 this constructed \(\epsilon/W\)-centered point uses \(\mu^{(\mathrm{init})}=100m^2W^4\epsilon^{-1}\).

We now run our interior point method on this initial point with target parameter \(\mu^{(\mathrm{target})}=\epsilon/(Wn^2)\). Theorem 4.1 tells us we need \(\widetilde{O}(n^{1.5}\mathrm{log}(W/\epsilon)\) space and \(\widetilde{O}(\sqrt{n}\mathrm{log}(W/\epsilon))\) passes to construct the resulting flow _x_. The total runtime is \(\widetilde{O}(mn\mathrm{log}^2(W/\epsilon))\).

The constructed flow \(x\in\mathbb{R}^E\) is not an optimal min-cost flow, since (i) the cost is slightly too large, (ii) the flow might use the star-edge that were added in the initial point construction.

However, by Lemma 6.4 and accuracy parameter \(\epsilon/W\) we see that for \(\mu^{(\mathrm{target})}=\epsilon/(Wn^2)\) our constructed _x_ is such that there exists an optimal solution _x_<sup>OPT</sup> with \(\|x-x^\mathrm{OPT}\|_\infty\le\epsilon\). In particular, this optimal flow does not route any flow on the star-edges that were added in the initial point construct, so _x_<sup>OPT</sup> is an optimal flow for the original input graph.

Thus _f=x_<sup>OPT</sup> is the optimal flow, and <o>_f_</o>=_x_ is the flow for which we can query edges, and we have \(\|f-\overline{f}\|_\infty\le\epsilon\).

By Theorem 4.1, we use \(\widetilde{O}(n^{1.5}\mathrm{log}(W/\epsilon))\) space with query time on each edge \(\widetilde{O}(\sqrt{n}\mathrm{log}(W/\epsilon))\).

Next, we analyze our algorithm in the context of communication complexity. In this setting, we are only limited by the number of bits sent between the players and not the bits stored, so we can use the following isolation lemma to find an exact flow.

**Lemma 6.8** (\[DS08b\], or Lemma 7.8 of \[BLL<sup>+</sup>21\])**.** _Let_ \(\Pi=(G,b,c)\) _be an instance for minimum-cost flow problem where G is a directed graph with m edges, the demand vector_ \(b\in\{-W,\dots,W\}^V\)_, the cost vector_ \(c\in\{-W,\dots,W\}^E\) _and the capacity vector_ \(u\in\{0,\dots,W\}^E\).

_Let the perturbed instance_ \(\Pi'=(G,b,c')\) _be such that_ \(c'_e=c_e+z_e\) _where z<sub>e</sub> is a random number from the set_ \(\big\{\frac{1}{4m^2W^2},\dots,\frac{2mW}{4m^2W^2}\big\}\)_. Let_ \(x'\) _be a feasible flow for_ \(\Pi'\) _whose cost is at most_ \(\mathrm{OPT}(\Pi')+\frac{1}{12m^2W^3}\) _where_ \(\mathrm{OPT}(\Pi')\) _is the optimal cost for problem_ \(\Pi'\)_. With probability at least 1/2, there exists an optimal feasible and integral flow x for_ \(\Pi\) _such that_ \(\|x-x'\|_\infty\le 1/3\).

**Theorem 1.2** (Min-cost Flow, Communication)**.** _There is a randomized communication protocol that, given an input graph G_=(_V,E_) _with integer capacities_ \(u\in[1,W]^E\) _and costs_ \(c\in[1,W]^E\)_, vertex demand vector_ \(b\in\mathbb{R}^V\)_, where the edges are split among two parties, computes w.h.p. an exact min-cost flow in_ \(\widetilde{O}(n^{1.5}\mathrm{log}(W))\) _communication. At the end, each party knows the amount of flow on each of their own edges._

_Proof._ Theorem 1.2 follows directly in the same way as Theorem 1.1 while applying Theorem 4.19. For this, we need to show that we can indeed start with \((x^{(\mathrm{init})},s^{(\mathrm{init})},\mu^{(\mathrm{init})})\) for both parties. \(\mu^{(\mathrm{init})}\) is a single predetermined scalar, and for all non-star edges _e_, each party can query \(x_e^{(\mathrm{init})}\) and \(s_e^{(\mathrm{init})}\) for their own edge set, because they are simply _u<sub>e</sub>/_2 and _c<sub>e</sub>_, respectively, which each party knows for their own edge set. The flow on star edges \(e'\) depend on the vertex demands: since there are only _O(n_) vertices each party can add up the demands from their edges and send them to each other using _O(n_) bits of communication to both know the total vertex demands and determine \(x_{e^\prime}^{(\mathrm{init})}\) and \(s_{e^\prime}^{(\mathrm{init})}\).

Then, we can continue with the algorithm (Theorem 4.19) until both parties know the final transcript. Then each party can query the flow on their own edge set via the transcript.

Now, to make the flow unique, we very slightly modify the above algorithm. Before we do anything we first perturb the edge costs _c_ to \(c'\) according to Lemma 6.8. Storing the perturbations takes _O(m_) space, which can be larger than _O(n_<sup>1.5</sup>), but crucially neither player needs to send this information to the other, as each keeps track of its own edges.

Then, after completing the algorithm, consider our final flow \(\left[\begin{array}{ c }
x^{(\mathrm{final})} \\
\widetilde{x}^{(\mathrm{final})}
\end{array}\right]\). Lemma 6.8 guarantees with probability at least 1/2 that there exists an integral min cost flow \(\left[\begin{array}{ c }
x^\mathrm{OPT} \\
\widetilde{x}^\mathrm{OPT}
\end{array}\right]\) with each edge value differing by at most 1/3. Thus, with probability at least 1/2, if whenever we query the flow along an edge we instead round it to the nearest integer, we recover \(\left[\begin{array}{ c }
x^\mathrm{OPT} \\
\widetilde{x}^\mathrm{OPT}
\end{array}\right]\). Moreover, by Lemma 6.7, we know \(\|\widetilde{x}^{(\mathrm{final})}\|_\infty\) can be made less than 1/3, so indeed the rounded \(\left[\begin{array}{ c }
x^{(\mathrm{final})} \\
\widetilde{x}^{(\mathrm{final})}
\end{array}\right]\) is feasible and optimal for _G_.

Finally, we can boost our success probability to high probability by resampling the random edge costs and repeating our algorithm _O_(log_n_) times. We can verify whether each flow is feasible after rounding by comparing the vertex demands, i.e., having both parties exchange the net-flow on each vertex and checking that they add up to demand _b_, which uses _O(n_) space. Then in the end, we choose the flow with the smallest cost among all constructed feasible flows.

## Remark on Bit-complexity

The bit complexity can be bounded via the following lemma from \[BLL<sup>+</sup>21\].

**Lemma 6.9** (\[BLL<sup>+</sup>21, Lemma 4.46\])**.** _Let W be the ratio of largest to smallest entry of_ \(\phi''(x^{(\mathrm{init})})^{1/2}\) _and let_ \(W'\) _be the ratio of largest to smallest entry of_ \(\phi''(x)^{1/2}\) _encountered throughout the algorithm. Then_

\(\mathrm{log}W'=\widetilde{O}(\mathrm{log}W+\mathrm{log}(1/\mu^{(\mathrm{final})})+\mathrm{log}\|c\|_\infty).\)

Observe that by \(\phi''(x)=1/x_e^2-1/(u_e-x_e)^2\) and \(\mu^{(\mathrm{final})}=1/(\epsilon\cdot\mathrm{poly}(n\mathrm{max}(\|c\|_\infty,\|u\|_\infty,\|b\|_\infty)))\), this lemma bounds the smallest value we might obtain for any _x<sub>e</sub>_. If we run the algorithm with some \(\widetilde{O}(\mathrm{log}(W/\epsilon))\) bit-length numbers, then we can guarantee the additive error is small multiplicative error, i.e., we always have a multiplicative approximation of _x_ and \(\phi''(x)\). Since the algorithm supports spectral approximation of \(\mathbf{A}^\top\mathbf{T}^{-1}\Phi''(x)^{-1}\mathbf{A}\), and entry-wise approximation of \(\tau\) and _x_ when defining _g_ (Section 5), the algorithm and<tbc>
